{"passages":"passage 1:The cause of irritable bowel syndrome is currently unknown. It is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea.\n\npassage 2:The exact cause of irritable bowel syndrome is unknown. It is believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. The following are risk factors thought to cause IBS: 1  Abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong) 2  Hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas. 3  Food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\n\npassage 3:People with IBS have symptoms that can include: 1  Diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea) 2  Constipation. 3  Constipation alternating with diarrhea.  Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel 1  movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\n\n","question":"symptoms and causes of ibs"}

CANDIDATE RESPONSE:
The symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea, constipation, and alternating episodes of both. Individuals with IBS may also experience belly pains or cramps, especially in the lower half of the belly, which worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement. Increased gas and bloating are also common symptoms. The exact cause of IBS is currently unknown, but it is believed to result from a combination of factors such as abnormal gastrointestinal tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in brain-GI tract communication, alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.
